<h1>Help</h1>
<p class="lede-inline">
  Quick orientation for the encryption-ish topics the app links to. Flim is a
  modified version of Element X, so for the deep dives we send you to Element’s
  docs instead of rewriting a whole manual.
</p>

<p class="credit credit-inline">
  If you’re wondering who made Flim and why the app cares about voice messages
  and one-handed use, start with <a href="/about">about</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="encryption">Encryption</h2>
<p>
  Chats are encrypted so only the people in the conversation can read the
  messages — not the service in the middle (unless a chat specifically is set to
  non-encrypted, or you choose a homeserver that does not support that). That’s
  a good thing for privacy. It can get a bit awkward if you switch phones or
  lose access to an old device; the links below explain how to stay out of
  trouble.
</p>
<p>
  For a fuller walkthrough, see
  <a href="https://element.io/help#encryption">Element’s encryption help</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="encryption-device-verification">Device verification</h2>
<p>
  Verification is how you (and the people you chat with) build confidence that
  a session really belongs to who it claims. It’s also how you mark your own
  phones and laptops as trusted so cross-signing can do its job.
</p>
<p>
  Details:
  <a href="https://element.io/help#encryption-device-verification">Element’s device verification help</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="encryption5">Chat backup</h2>
<p>
  Secure backup is the “new phone, don’t lose the ability to read old encrypted
  history” safety net. Set it up before you need it; future-you will be less
  grumpy.
</p>
<p>
  Details:
  <a href="https://element.io/help#encryption5">Element’s chat backup help</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="encryption18">Identity pinning</h2>
<p>
  If someone’s cryptographic identity suddenly looks different, pinning-related
  warnings are there so you notice — maybe they reset a device; maybe something
  weirder is going on. Don’t sleepwalk past those prompts.
</p>
<p>
  Details:
  <a href="https://element.io/help#encryption18">Element’s identity pinning help</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="e2ee-history-sharing">History sharing</h2>
<p>
  Sharing encrypted history with a new device or a newly invited person depends
  on key sharing and chat settings. It’s not always automatic, and that’s often
  on purpose.
</p>
<p>
  Details:
  <a href="https://element.io/en/help#e2ee-history-sharing">Element’s history sharing help</a>.
</p>

<h2>Still stuck?</h2>
<p>
  Encryption topics get dense fast. If the Element docs don’t match what you see
  in Flim, that’s useful feedback — the UI may have moved, or we may have
  simplified something. The <a href="/about">about</a> page has the human
  context for who’s listening.
</p>
